\section{Results and Analysis}

The ability of Perception Trend Analysis to identify the shift correctly and formulate factual responses is dependent on the information retrieved by SerApi. It correlates the shifts to the news articles in that time frame to identify the root causes behind it. The following are the evaluation methodologies we employed to cross-verify model outputs.

\subsection{Groundedness and Factual Fidelity
}
The LLM-generated narrative report and its correlation with the retrieved News Articles are evaluated here. The Groundedness is a measure of how much of the report is explicitly supported by the raw text snippets retrieved from the SERP API. A hybrid scoring system is used to measure the quality of the LLM generated report:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Claim Decomposition:} The report is segmented into sentence-level assertions.

    \item \textbf{Evidence Windowing:}Raw text is partitioned into overlapping five-sentence windows to preserve context.

    \item \textbf{NLI Classification:} A specialized cross-encoder evaluates candidate pairs for logical entailment.
\end{enumerate}

A claim is defined as grounded if it satisfies either the Natural Language Inference (NLI) entailment probability threshold ($P_{NLI} \geq 0.30$) or the cosine similarity threshold ($S_{cos} \geq 0.30$). It was observed in Fig. ~\ref{fig:groundedness_analysis} that utilizing higher baselines (e.g., 0.50) unfairly penalized valid, high-level LLM recommendations by misclassifying them as hallucinations.

